    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d518">

                <pb facs="0618" n="550" sameAs="frus:frus1914-20v01/pg550"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">851.857 Su 8/82½</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        President Wilson
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-04-15">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 15, 1916</hi>.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. President</hi>: I have been through the
                    affidavits which were taken in France in the case of the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Sussex</hi>, and also some that were taken in England.<anchor n="38"
                        xml:id="d518-f1"/> They do not add materially to our fund of knowledge in
                    regard to the case, although they do furnish three additional witnesses who saw
                    the wake of the torpedo as it approached the vessel.</p>
                <p>I am enclosing a redraft of the statement of facts, giving the
                        authorities.<anchor n="39" xml:id="d518-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>Faithfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d518-f1" n="38" xml:id="d518-f1-content">
                <p>For text of these affidavits, see <hi rendition="#italic">Diplomatic
                        Correspondence With Belligerent Governments Relating to Neutral Rights and
                        Duties</hi> (Washington, Government Printing Office, 1916), vol. III, pp.
                    251 ff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d518-f2" n="39" xml:id="d518-f2-content">
                <p>For the statement as sent, see <ref target="frus1916Supp#pg_234">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1916, supp., p.
                    234</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
